::: {.doc-subject}

Traverse outward node-by-node until stopping conditions are met.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33151-L33399){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.trav_out_until(
    conditions,
    max_steps=30,
    exclude_unmatched=True,
    add_to_selection=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Outward traversal until a condition](../assets/graphs/trav-out-until.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: node C is selected in a seven-node directed graph whose nodes carry values; the condition is value at least 5. Right: walking forward from C through B and D, the first match is F (value 7), which becomes the selection. G also matches but is never reached, because the walk stops at F; A matches but lies against the arrows." width="100%"}

From each currently selected node, walks forward along outgoing edges in breadth-first
order and stops each branch at the first node that satisfies `conditions=`. Matching nodes
are not expanded further, so anything beyond them is never visited. Branches that never
match keep going until they run out of outgoing edges or until `max_steps=` hops have been
taken. When the walk finishes, the node selection is replaced by the result.

This is useful for:

- finding the nearest downstream node of a given kind (e.g., the first "approval" step
  after a task, or the first warehouse reachable from a supplier)
- locating the boundary where an attribute crosses a threshold along directed paths
- collecting everything traversed on the way to such a boundary (with
  `add_to_selection=True, exclude_unmatched=False`)

Things to keep in mind:

- each starting node is traversed independently; the results are combined
- a starting node that already satisfies the condition is kept as-is and not expanded
- in undirected graphs every neighbor counts as an outward neighbor, so the walk spreads
  in all directions
- if no nodes are selected, the method does nothing
- `conditions=` is a Python callable invoked once per visited node (and again on some nodes
  when building the final selection), so very large traversals are bounded by Python
  speed
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[conditions]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [callable]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A callable that receives a node ID (an `int`) and returns `True` when the walk should
    stop at that node. Node attributes can be read inside the callable, e.g., with
    `graph.get_node(node_id).attributes`. The callable is evaluated on the starting nodes
    too.

<code>[max_steps]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[30]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Maximum number of hops taken outward from each starting node. Default is `30`. When a
    branch reaches this depth without a match, the last frontier of unmatched nodes is
    what `exclude_unmatched=False` will keep (see below).

<code>[exclude_unmatched]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Whether nodes that do not satisfy `conditions=` may end up in the final selection.
    If `True` (the default), only matching nodes are selected, so a start node whose
    branches never match contributes nothing. If `False` and `add_to_selection=` is
    `False`, the deepest unmatched nodes reached (the frontier at `max_steps=`, or the
    dead-end level where the walk ran out of edges) are selected alongside the matches.
    If `False` and `add_to_selection=` is `True`, every visited node is added.

<code>[add_to_selection]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Whether to select the traversed path or just its endpoints. If `False` (the
    default), the selection becomes the matching nodes (plus the unmatched frontier when
    `exclude_unmatched=False`). If `True`, the current selection (the starting nodes) is
    kept and nodes visited during the walk are added to it; with the default
    `exclude_unmatched=True` only the visited nodes that match are added, so pair it with
    `exclude_unmatched=False` to add the whole traversed region.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~trav_in_until`) 

:   Walk backward until a node passes a test.

[](`~trav_out`) 

:   Move the selection along outgoing edges.

[](`~descendants`) 

:   The nodes reachable from a node.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Each node here has a `value`. Walking forward from `A` until a node has a value of 5 or
more stops at `E` on one branch and at `D` on the other. `F` has a large value too, but
it lies past `E`, so the walk never gets there:

::: {#b73a6044 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("B", "E"), ("C", "D"), ("E", "F")],
    directed=True,
)
values = {"A": 1, "B": 2, "C": 3, "D": 9, "E": 7, "F": 8}
graph.set_node_attrs("value", values)

def is_big(node):
    return graph.get_node(node).attributes["value"] >= 5

label = graph.get_label_from_id
start = graph.get_id_from_label("A")
graph.select_nodes_by_id([start]).trav_out_until(is_big)

sorted(label(n) for n in graph.get_selected_node_ids())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['D', 'E']
```
:::
:::


Each node labeled with its value, and the two where the walk stopped in a second color:

::: {#4fe7b362 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {
    "A": (0, 1), "B": (1, 1), "C": (2, 2),
    "D": (3, 2), "E": (2, 0), "F": (3, 0),
}
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)
nodes = graph.get_node_ids()
chosen = set(graph.get_selected_node_ids())
graph.set_node_attrs("stopped", {n: n in chosen for n in nodes})
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "tag", {n: f"{label(n)} ({values[label(n)]})" for n in nodes}
)

graph.plot(layout="pos", color_by="stopped", label_by="tag", height=240)
```

